5. Non-Functional Requirements
5.1 Security
NFR-001: The system must enforce authentication for protected functions.
NFR-002: Authorisation must be enforced on the backend.
NFR-003: Users must not access or modify records they are not authorised to access.
NFR-004: Passwords must not be stored in plaintext.
NFR-005: Application traffic must use HTTPS.
NFR-006: Secrets must not be committed to source control.
NFR-007: File uploads must be validated.
NFR-008: Important security and administrative events must be logged.
5.2 Performance
NFR-009: Common search and listing requests should target a 95th-percentile response time below three seconds under the agreed pilot load.
NFR-010: Database queries must be optimised for frequently used filters.
NFR-011: Large result sets must support pagination.
NFR-012: Long-running processes must not block ordinary user interactions.
5.3 Availability
NFR-013: The production service should target 99.5% monthly availability after launch, subject to infrastructure capability.
NFR-014: The system must have a documented backup strategy.
NFR-015: The team must have a documented recovery process.
5.4 Usability
NFR-016: The application must be responsive.
NFR-017: Core workflows must work on mobile devices.
NFR-018: Forms must provide clear validation messages.
NFR-019: Interfaces must use understandable language.
NFR-020: Important actions must provide clear success and failure feedback.
5.5 Scalability
NFR-021: The platform must support additional crops without redesigning core tables.
NFR-022: The platform must support additional participant roles through configurable permissions.
NFR-023: The system must support expansion into additional geographic locations.
NFR-024: The architecture must allow new modules to be introduced without rewriting the entire application.
5.6 Maintainability
NFR-025: Business logic must be separated from presentation components.
NFR-026: Core business rules must be testable.
NFR-027: Database schema changes must be version-controlled.
NFR-028: Application interfaces and major modules must be documented.
5.7 Data Integrity
NFR-029: Every quantity must be associated with a unit.
NFR-030: Every supply and procurement record must reference a valid crop.
NFR-031: Records must maintain ownership information.
NFR-032: Important status transitions must be validated.
NFR-033: Aggregated quantities must retain traceable source records.

6. Core Business Rules
Every supply listing must reference a supported crop.
Every procurement request must reference a supported crop.
Quantity must always be stored with its unit.
Different units must not be compared without a valid conversion rule.
Expected supply must be distinguishable from currently available supply.
Potential matches must not be treated as confirmed transactions.
Only authorised participants can modify their records.
Organisation-level actions must respect membership permissions.
Aggregated produce must retain source references.
Closed or expired listings must not appear as active supply.
Verification status must only be assigned through the approved verification process.
Users must be able to report suspicious or misleading content.
Commercial and personal information must respect access-control rules.
The system must not guarantee quality, delivery or payment simply because a participant uses RiceLink.

7. Initial Release Acceptance Criteria
The first multi-crop release is ready for controlled testing when:
Users can register and authenticate.
Users can create appropriate profiles.
The platform supports the approved participant roles.
Administrators can manage the crop catalogue.
The system supports multiple units of measurement.
Producers can publish and manage supply listings.
Buyers and processors can publish and manage procurement requests.
Users can search and filter supply and demand.
The system can display potential matches.
Users can initiate and manage enquiries.
Permissions are enforced on the backend.
Administrators can moderate reported content.
Core workflows work on mobile devices.
Critical security and data-integrity tests pass.

8. Open Questions for Product and Engineering
Which crops should be included in the first operational pilot?
Which exact LGAs will the initial pilot cover?
Which measurement units are required for each selected crop?
What information should be mandatory for each participant role?
What verification process should apply to organisations?
Will communication initially happen within RiceLink or through external contact details?
Should deal/order management be included in the first release?
Which notification channels should be supported?
What should the first commercial revenue experiment test?
What agricultural stakeholders will participate in product validation?
